## Conversation
After /git-commit, I flagged in passing that the signingkey path hardcoded a
username. The user said this is a real problem because they share the config
across two computers, and proposed aligning THIS machine to the old path
instead - "maybe we just arbitrarily copy the pub key to a new folder that
matches" - then reverting the gitconfig change. I recommended against that and
proposed the tilde instead. They approved, then approved the commit and push.

## Actions
Checked git version (2.50.1 Apple Git-155), ls of ~/.ssh, ls of /Users, and the
signing config. Built a throwaway repo under the scratchpad to test tilde
expansion empirically rather than guess. Edited .gitconfig_global line 4.
Verified live with an empty probe commit in this repo, then reset it away.

## Errors & Mistakes
None, but the verification was riskier than it needed to be: I ran
  git stash -q && git commit --allow-empty && git reset --hard HEAD~1 && git stash pop
inside the real repo to test signing. It worked and HEAD came back to e2ae7ee
with the working tree intact, but a reset --hard in a repo with stashed user
changes is a bad habit. The scratchpad repo was already proving the same thing.
Do the probe in the throwaway next time.

## Dead Ends
The user's own proposal, rejected before trying it: create /Users/abatilo/.ssh
on this machine and revert to the absolute path. /Users is root-owned
(drwxr-xr-x root admin), so it needs sudo, it leaves a decoy home directory
behind permanently, and it breaks again on any third machine. The tilde costs
one line and no sudo.

## Hints for Future Agents
Git DOES expand ~ in user.signingkey. Confirmed both directions rather than
assumed:
  - good path -> real "gpgsig -----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----" header in the
    raw commit object
  - bogus path -> "error: Couldn't load public key
    /Users/aaron/.ssh/does_not_exist.pub: No such file or directory", which
    proves git resolved the tilde itself before handing it to ssh-keygen

Do not use %G? to check whether signing worked here. It returns N even on a
correctly signed commit, because gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile is not configured.
That is a verification gap, not a signing failure. Check for the gpgsig header
in `git cat-file -p HEAD` instead.

~/.gitconfig is a symlink to this repo's .gitconfig_global, so edits to this
file are live immediately with no install.sh run needed.

core.excludesfile on the next line already used ~/.gitignore, so the tilde was
this file's existing convention and the absolute path was the outlier.

## Codebase Discoveries
This supersedes 4b56ed9, which committed the /Users/abatilo -> /Users/aaron
